Make split's -b and -l mutually exclusive.
Both options stored their argument in the same variable, so with both
given, the input was split by bytes using whichever argument came last,
and that argument was also parsed as a line count: -l 5 -b 1k failed
with "invalid number '1k'", while -b 1k -l 5 made 5-byte pieces. GNU
split rejects the combination; show the usage message instead.

---
 coreutils/split.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/split.c
+++ b/coreutils/split.c
@@ -108,7 +108,7 @@
 
 	opt = getopt32(argv, "^"
 			"l:b:a:+" /* -a N */
-			"\0" "?2"/*max 2 args*/,
+			"\0" "?2"/*max 2 args*/ ":l--b:b--l",
 			&count_p, &count_p, &suffix_len
 	);
 
